09:33:40 [evaluator]   R1 9/10 (evaluator): Every sentence is in the present tense and nothing slips into third person. But one outcome never puts the Tinker in the scene at all: it is narration with no "you" in it.
09:33:40 [evaluator]   R2 9/10 (evaluator): The details are concrete and mostly specific to this game: spirit stove, dented pot, scorched aprons, an unlabelled tin, a pressure table. There are two small lapses. The cupboard in the label never appears in the description. The "No. 4 spanner" is the style guide's own example copied word for word, so it reads as ticking a box rather than as something seen.
09:33:40 [evaluator]   R3 8/10 (evaluator): The wit is dry and often lands ("an example of incomplete combustion", "the only person present who has been seen to cook"). But "which is worse" tells the reader how to take the moment instead of trusting it. The pensions simile is a shade broad. The grim side is thin: the only hardship is a missed supper.
09:33:40 [evaluator]   R6 9/10 (evaluator): Nothing is explained. But a ration "wrapped in a page of laboratory notes" at the Polytechnic invites careful readers to see a Hester fragment. The brief rules out her laboratory, and the scene does not need it.
09:33:40 [evaluator]   R8 9/10 (evaluator): The scene is built on canon: the basement practical bench and the qualified hand who tests each boiler before it is lit. It uses only existing items and has no Crowns. Onions are new fresh produce in a city with no ground. That is small and arguably covered by barley being canon, but nothing in the scene draws on Kettleport's own food, like tea or the public urn.
09:33:40 [evaluator]   R10 9/10 (evaluator): Odds: P = 0.6 × skill / 7, so Cooking 3 to 8 gives 26%, 34%, 43%, 51%, 60% and 69%, a mean of about 47%. That is "about half", and no whole-number difficulty does better (6 gives a mean of 55%). The three options cost and risk different things. But option 3 pays exactly what option 1 pays on a success. That makes it just "pay pork for certainty", and the argument comes down to an exchange rate rather than a real choice.
